#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561089} :activity/id #uuid "0ac10cd6-87b4-5be4-8d2a-8b946ef63557" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050409 :topic/id #uuid "46beb5e4-992f-546d-8051-796c949740a1" :topic/math-academy-id 526} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cdc61b3-8b79-5ea8-a619-8426af491629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339670} {:answer/id #uuid "67f37f95-6de1-5d04-b71e-ed84f5ca8a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340056} {:answer/id #uuid "ab06bd68-2d98-5de6-8d36-5e85b3672d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340684} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ad83ea-b045-52c1-bf05-b58e0937ea33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340869} {:answer/id #uuid "e8141639-7376-52d2-962a-0f9bff8db53d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341176}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341176} :answer-field/id #uuid "e40ef719-3a96-5536-9367-e13d22ca4b80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5a03ec8c-4418-5e4a-aaf2-129c88104ba7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74435" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-3,-6)$ and the line $y+2x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2x & =-2 \\\\ y & =-2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/78/789659bd700bc5f8407d409bc28bf5ce5309369af2aa5ad5973434d47cff74fa.png)\n\n \n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-2x-2$ and passes through the point $(-3,-6).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-2,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+3) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=-2x-2 \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -2x-\\frac{1}{2}x & =2-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}x & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-4.$ So, our solution is $x=1$ and $y=-4.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(1,-4).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-3,-6)$ and $(1,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(1-(-3))}^{2}+(-4-(-6))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-3,-6)$ and the line $y+2x=-2$ is $2\\sqrt{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "581eac97-d55b-546b-8351-221450ce3692" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561096}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19034fbf-ecd9-55d6-861b-0d5e5661524d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339349} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddbc882-a5c7-5c65-8d64-d4d3043edf48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339533} {:answer/id #uuid "4add45f8-1363-5fe9-be2e-64e9fe43e788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339801} {:answer/id #uuid "83bfa442-6fa5-5f09-aec0-57fb97c3556e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340313} {:answer/id #uuid "b1385a23-f178-5808-937d-bee3d508b96e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339533} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f5e6af3-06a0-5b37-9c66-995c74e7a84d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac99e412-cc1f-5160-a402-dce6424f38c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-74420" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(5,-8)$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/6e/6ec04fe0ad996f87dbc07b0883afb3fa7aef1429412a550afe9102d1c22db71b.png)\n\n  \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3$ and passes through the point $(5,-8).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{1}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-3.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+8 & =-3(x-5) \\\\ y+8 & =-3x+15 \\\\ y & =-3x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{1}{3}x-3 \\\\ y &=-3x+7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x-3 & =-3x+7 \\\\ 3x+\\frac{1}{3}x & =7+3 \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =10 \\\\ x & =10\\cdot \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,-2).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(5,-8)$ and $(3,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-5)^{2}+(-2-(-8))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(5,-8)$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3$ is $2\\sqrt{10}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b8b9a1c0-92da-5136-9409-0bd8db22ebdf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561074}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f7ce0c-16e5-531a-94b9-547622df3f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339767} {:answer/id #uuid "569cbb0a-f99c-5355-8f53-bdc678082993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339923} {:answer/id #uuid "8688ee8a-8219-5c84-a042-632559c1a6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340337} {:answer/id #uuid "95f977c5-80d6-5f10-bfe2-b1560cf968b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340481} {:answer/id #uuid "ae765e2d-062d-5b17-8c52-1eef7d4ff32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-5,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340481} :answer-field/id #uuid "73585de0-1b70-548d-9d76-12f3a61d673b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7220b7e3-9cce-5102-874c-014511b9e7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-74439" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d687bcdc23c283e0945b039781450d2a020f0fd89c0d2976bb252914b296978.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+x & =-4 \\\\ y & =-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x-4$ and passes through the point $(0,2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =x \\\\ y & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=x+2 \\\\ y &=-x-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =-x-4 \\\\ x+x & =-4-2 \\\\ 2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-1.$ So, our solution is $x=-3$ and $y=-1.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2)$ is  $(-3,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "d242eb63-36df-5068-8499-2c2f42772c0c"}] :activity/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560351}]}
